# PowerShell shell (Windows)

The agent shell on this setup is PowerShell. Do not use bash-only constructs in it.

## Forbidden in PowerShell

- Heredoc: `$(cat <<'EOF' ... EOF)`
- Unquoted git upstream: `@{u}` (PowerShell parses `@{...}` as a hashtable). Write `'@{u}'`.
- `export FOO=bar`, bash arrays, `&&` chains that assume bash semantics for `$?`.

## Use instead

- Multi-line text for a commit: a here-string.

```powershell
git commit -m @"
type(scope): subject

body
"@
```

  `git commit -F -` with a here-string does not work: PowerShell passes the text as an argument and git reads it as a pathspec. Use `-m` as above, or commit from Bash with a heredoc.
- Long text for `gh`: write a temporary UTF-8 file and pass `--body-file`, then delete it (`.cursor/rules/scratch.mdc`).
- The output of a native command is an array of lines in PowerShell. Join it before editing text read back from `gh` (``(gh pr view 63 --json body --jq .body) -join "`n"``): `.Replace` on the array and `WriteAllText` of it join the lines with spaces, and PR #63's body lost its line breaks that way.
- Do not put `Remove-Item` in the same command as `$env:PATH += ';C:\Program Files ...'`: the agent's PowerShell tool reads `C:\Program` as the item to remove and refuses the whole command. Remove files in a separate call, or from Bash.
- A command that holds `C:\Program Files (x86)\...` (the `vswhere` path a NativeAOT publish adds to `PATH`) and calls `Remove-Item` is refused by the same check as a removal of a system path, and none of it runs. Clean up in a command of its own.
- `Remove-Item` in a command that also holds quoted text (a commit message in a here-string, the arguments of `.Replace`) is refused as a removal of a system path (`/` or `//`), and none of the command runs. In PR #67, a commit and an edit of the PR's body did not run for it, twice. Remove temporary files in a command of their own, or from Bash.
- `Remove-Item` with a wildcard under the scratchpad (`...\scratchpad\*`) is refused as a removal of a system path. Remove scratch files from Bash, naming each file.
- A helper script (Python, a mutant patch, a data generator): write it to a file in the scratchpad first, then run it. Do not build it from a here-string piped to `Set-Content` in the same command that removes files: the tool can read a line break of the here-string as a path for `Remove-Item` and refuse the whole command.
- A Python script that prints text outside ASCII (Japanese from the design, `≥`) runs as `python -X utf8 -I <script>`. Under the console's code page (cp932 on this machine), `print` throws `UnicodeEncodeError`, and setting `PYTHONIOENCODING` does not help, because `-I` ignores it. In PR #82, a script that printed a line of the design failed this way.
- Inside an array, the comma binds before `+`: `@('old', 'a' + "`n" + 'b')` is not the pair it looks like, and the text after the line break (`'b'`) is dropped from the replacement. In PR #77, a sentence of design §7.5 and an item of §4.9 were lost this way, and the PR body claimed what was not in the file. Put each joined string in parentheses, or build it in a variable first. After a scripted replacement, count each added text in the file (`grep -c`), one at a time.
- Native commands set `$LASTEXITCODE`. Check it before the next step: `dotnet test ...; if ($LASTEXITCODE -ne 0) { exit $LASTEXITCODE }`.
- Variable names are not case-sensitive. A local `$low` is the parameter `$Low`: a loop that sets `$low` overwrites the parameter, and a measurement that depends on it does not end. Give locals names that differ from every parameter in more than case (`$fewer`, not `$low`).
- Splatting one string (`& $app @args` where `$args` is `'--points'`) passes its characters one by one. Build arguments as an array: `[string[]]$arguments = @(...)`. Runs that drop their scene's arguments this way still produce figures, of the wrong scene.
- Run `./build.ps1` in PowerShell 7 (`pwsh`, the agent's PowerShell tool), not Windows PowerShell 5.1 (`powershell.exe`): 5.1 has no `$IsWindows`, which the gate reads.
- A scratch build that reads an environment variable is run from Bash, with `VAR=value cmd` and `env -u VAR cmd`. After `[Environment]::SetEnvironmentVariable(name, $null)` in PowerShell, child processes were seen to still get the variable (cause not traced), and runs meant to differ came out the same.
- Quote paths with spaces. Prefer repository-relative paths, except for .NET methods: `[IO.File]::ReadAllText` and the like read a relative path from the process's working directory, not from `Set-Location`. Pass them an absolute path; otherwise a replacement in a worktree reads the main checkout's file and reports its text missing.
- In Bash, `rm -rf "$DIR"/*` is refused by the agent's safety check, because an empty `$DIR` would make it `/*`. Write `"${DIR:?}"/*` or a literal path.

## Writing files

Write files with the editor tools, not with shell redirection. A Python script sent through the Bash tool loses the backslashes of `\f`, `\r`, `\t`, and `\v` in its string literals: a path such as `static\file.lib` does not match, and a carriage return is written into a path that held `\r`. Edit text that holds backslashes with the editor tool. A script that another script writes is written with the editor tool too: nested quoting fails in ways that only show after a long run, such as the byte 0 itself written in place of a C# escape, which builds and then makes `grep` take the file for a binary.

After an edit by a script, count the control bytes in the files it wrote with `tr -cd '\000-\010\013\014\016-\037' < <file> | wc -c`; the count is 0. `grep -cP '[\x00-\x08\x0B\x0C\x0E-\x1F]'` counts no byte 0, so it misses the worst case.

The Edit tool does not match a line that holds a `\uXXXX` escape in a C# string, in either form, and a Python script sent through Bash loses the backslash of `\\u` and does not run. Edit the ranges before and after the line, and write a script that holds such text to a file first.

`Copy-Item` keeps the file's last write time, so an incremental build does not see a file put back from a copy and keeps the old output: a shader variant copied over the source is not built, and the other variant is measured. After copying a source file into place, set its time to now (`(Get-Item <file>).LastWriteTime = Get-Date`).

```powershell
# BAD — one string splatted: the app receives '-', '-', 'p', ...
$args = '--points 100000000'
& $app @args

# GOOD
[string[]]$arguments = @('--points', '100000000')
& $app @arguments
```
